==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[neoterical]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{term|neotericus}}, from Hellenistic Greek {{term|νεωτερικός|lang=grc}}, from comparative of {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|νέος||new|lang=grc}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/niːə(ʊ)ˈtɛɹɪk/|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Modern, [[new-fangled]].
#* Fitzed. Hall
#*: Our '''neoteric''' verbs.''
# New; recent.
#*"Should it all come crashing in on us . . . will there be enough luddites, whose hands remember, to free us from the chains of '''neoteric''' technology?" &mdash; ''The Toronto Star'', August 21, 1998
#*"A few words on the two neoteric terms, ''[[cybertext]]'' and ''[[ergodic]]'', are in order." &mdash; ''Cybertext'', 1997, Espen Aarseth.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A modern author (especially as opposed to a classical writer).
#*{{RQ:RBrtn AntmyMlncly}}, Bk.I, New York, 2001, p.140:
#*:Galen himself writes promiscuously of them both by reason of their affinity; but most of our '''neoterics''' do handle them apart, whom I will follow in this treatise.
# Someone with new or modern ideas.

